# What never belongs in a build context — issue #139.
#
# Not a tidiness list. `apps/web/Dockerfile` is built from the repository root,
# because the application depends on three source-only workspace packages, and
# without this the context is the entire checkout: two `node_modules` trees, two
# build outputs and the git history. That is hundreds of megabytes sent to the
# daemon before the first instruction runs, and it is also how a stale `.next`
# from somebody's laptop ends up inside an image.

.git
.github
.claude

# Build output and installed dependencies. Both are produced inside the image.
node_modules
**/node_modules
apps/web/.next
apps/api/build
apps/api/.gradle
packages/*/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Local-only configuration and notes. `apps/api/config` holds machine-local
# Spring overrides; hesablar.txt is a credential note that is git-ignored too.
apps/api/config
hesablar.txt

# Environment files, at any depth. `apps/web/Dockerfile` copies the whole context
# into its build stage, and Next.js reads `.env`, `.env.local` and
# `.env.production` during `next build` -- so a developer's local file would not
# only travel to the daemon, it would be compiled into the image, and every
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` value in it shipped to every browser. Nothing in the build needs
# one: the image is configured by its environment at run time, and the values the
# build does need are passed as build arguments.
.env*
**/.env*

# Storybook's build, the Playwright cache, coverage. None of it runs in a
# container.
packages/ui/storybook-static
.playwright-mcp
coverage

# Documentation and images are read by people, not by a build.
docs
README.md
CLAUDE.md
